\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{=}7$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$, so a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content.
Even frontier LLMs exhibit comparable collapse beyond $\Kempirical$: per-depth recall at $d \geq 2$ ranges 8--38\% (vs.\ 20\% chance), indicating error redistribution rather than genuine depth recovery (Appendix).
Since all main results use prompt-chain (fewer distributional artifacts than retrain-chain), $\Kempirical{=}2$ establishes a detection floor; retrain-chain $\Kempirical{=}7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}) represents the upper bound.

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$) followed by rapid convergence, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$); the retrain-chain ($\lambda = 0.285$, no cliff) extends $\Kempirical$ to 7 via cumulative model-adaptation artifacts (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Two-tier ceiling and domain dependence.}
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ C4 $\geq$ Code $>$ Wikipedia) correlates with structural complexity (Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).
The classifier gap is itself domain-dependent, yielding a two-tier ceiling: model-free features (OBD) set $\Kempirical \in \{1,2,3\}$, while DeBERTa recovers $+0$ on C4, $+1$ on Wikipedia, and $+1$ on arXiv (Table~\ref{tab:deberta_arxiv}).
On C4, the gap is zero across 1.5B, 14B, and 32B scales, indicating that the web-text boundary reflects fundamental information loss rather than classifier limitation.
On structurally rich domains, contextualized representations capture depth signal (formulaic patterns, citation structure) that 15D statistics cannot encode, quantifying the marginal contribution of classifier power to discriminability.

